    "done_when": "Find where a reckon crew dispatch spends its time when it is slow, with a measured attribution.\n\nMeasured 2026-10-07, as the span from a run id's timestamp to its supervisor.json mtime:\n- most of about 40 dispatches since 03:10Z took 11–57 s;\n- two outliers: r-20261007T041913587977-new-work-names-no-lane took 445 s, and r-20261007T033111*-session-host-plugin-waits-silently took 1,316 s;\n- an earlier attempt of new-work-names-no-lane was killed by a 110 s timeout after it had written a \"starting\" pointer at +18 s, with no worktree or run directory.\nJev's pick took 1.15 s in the slow one, so the pick is not the cause. The node was healthy at the time: load 11 on 28 CPUs, 58–73% idle.\nThe slow dispatch declared 11 write paths, including five skill files, tests/test_crew.py and reckon/hooks/coordinator_obligations.py, and a done-when naming a gate population.\n\n1. Reproduce: time the phases of reckon crew dispatch for a node shaped like the slow one, against a temporary RECKON_HOME and a fixture or scratch clone, with a stub launcher; never launch a real worker.\n2. Measure per phase:\n   - node and scope claims, including peer-scope conflict checks over the live pointers;\n   - the gate-population and done-when validation (does it run test collection, and over which files?);\n   - the plan-review gate;\n   - worktree creation and provisioning;\n   - the repository tree snapshot;\n   - the scratch headroom check;\n   - the picker;\n   - the supervisor start.\n   Report a table with each phase's seconds, for the slow shape and for a small two-path node.\n3. Name the dominant phase with evidence, and say whether the cost scales with write-path count, test-file size, live-pointer count or repository size.\nWrite only logs and a report under the run directory; the coordinator lands the evidence. Propose a fix in the manifest's follow_ons; do not change product code.",
